{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1967 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e8d98a2b8905de1f70868d2738721dfaa614c6102bdee1d5e5cab3644c7bfd74" :edb/db-before-t 1966 :edb/db-after-t 1967 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535279 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:09:52.429Z" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133621 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n$\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y)$." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133621 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\cos{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y}= \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133621 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/db911dc99f33b91c66fb04d8b1e4301f.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= \\cos{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-\\sin y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = \\sin y\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)\\cos ydx + x\\sin ydy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(\\sin y - (-\\sin y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2\\sin ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}\n$$" 13194139535279 false] [17592186133621 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133622 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0)$, $(1,2)$ and $(0,2)$, traversed counterclockwise." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133622 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C} 4x^2y \\, \\mathrm{d}x+ 2y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y},$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(1,2)$ and $(0,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." 13194139535279 true] [17592186133622 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/bdec15081a2e1ef58dcb3545c680a71d.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= 4x^2y\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 2y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 4x^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)4x^{2}ydx + 2y^{2}dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(0 - 4x^{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2x}^{2}(-4x^{2})dydx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-4∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(2 - 2x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(1 - x)dx \\\\\n&=-8∫_{0}^{1}x^{2} - x^{3}dx \\\\\n&=-8[\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{x^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-8(\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\\n&=-8 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 false] [17592186133622 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= 4x^2y$ and $Q(x,y)= 2y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) : 0 \\leq x \\leq 1,\\, 2x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,dx+2{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{2}(-4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(2-2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & =-8{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133623 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133623 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0), (1,1)$ and $(-1,1),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}x\\sin{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+ (x^2\\cos{y}-y^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y}$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186133623 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2116/Source/Images/d31d071642487b14587e48bac2c69b6a.png)\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= x\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = x\\cos y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 2x\\cos y\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y): -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)x\\sin ydx + (x^{2}\\cos y - y^{2})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(2x\\cos y - x\\cos y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x\\cos ydA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{-y}^{y}x\\cos ydxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 false] [17592186133623 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/2fa3c84359cec5f5e0750552e5a4b24b20b8dbc3d65b909aea908e1505cfb5e3.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= x\\sin{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x^2\\cos{y}-y^2.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -y\\leq x\\leq y,\\, 0\\leq y \\leq 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dx+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341274 13194139535279 true] [17592186133805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341281 13194139535279 true] [17592186133805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341288 13194139535279 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341295 13194139535279 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341302 13194139535279 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341309 13194139535279 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341337 13194139535279 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341316 13194139535279 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341323 13194139535279 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341330 13194139535279 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341344 13194139535279 true] [17592186133990 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535279 true] [17592186133990 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. Express $∮_(C)xy^{2}dx + 2x^{2}ydy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133990 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0)$ and $(1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise, and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186133990 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/2e63a57e50b60d7f0c6a3ce12d164716e2cba71c6bd4c6df10460a35beba4209.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133991 1017 #edb/ref 17592186130509 13194139535279 false] [17592186133991 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341268 13194139535279 true] [17592186133991 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535279 true] [17592186133991 1100 "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0)$ and $(1, 1)$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133991 1100 "Consider the square $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,1),(1,0)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133991 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x-y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133996 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186133996 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,2)$ and $(-2,2),$ traversed counterclockwise." 13194139535279 true] [17592186133996 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $∮_(C)(e^{x} - 2x^{2}y)dx + (e^{y} + 2xy^{2})dy$, where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0, 0)$, $(2, 2)$ and $(-2, 2)$, traversed counterclockwise." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133996 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133997 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535279 true] [17592186133997 1100 "Consider the path $C$, in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$, and the region $D$ bounded by $C$. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$∮_(C)e^{x}\\cos 2ydx + e^{x}\\sin 2ydy = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$." 13194139535279 false] [17592186133997 1100 "Consider the path $C,$ in the plane, as the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise. Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186133997 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/c44b320dd7ba2824ae0024a3d77d0c6cd6e06fa5889e23879f3d6f464c3a80df.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,dx+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y-(-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341269 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341270 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341271 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341272 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341273 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341273 13194139535279 true] [17592186341268 1110 #uuid "05b7ee70-3230-5a5b-a1e1-f230a06a6b9e" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341269 1034 "2(y-x)" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341269 1111 #uuid "d1a4a1cf-3357-5778-8aeb-f2a2995fc5d1" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341270 1034 "2(x+y)" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341270 1111 #uuid "a3b378e1-1c6a-580e-afaf-70c538fd8c26" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341271 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{3}+{x}^{3})" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341271 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341271 1111 #uuid "e902bb33-7013-56f8-b26b-1c735f923cfc" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341272 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341272 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341272 1111 #uuid "d73c410e-4300-5aeb-9be0-6e9f964f3d3c" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341273 1034 "2(x-y)" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341273 1111 #uuid "4c34a244-11ca-5569-aa54-e5db0629f83e" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341275 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1089 "q-109111" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+3xy\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1113 #uuid "840761dd-7cb5-59cb-8d00-6023949589a0" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341274 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/0dfe132146c4095954d6b628b055317c975fed6ec429b9ad48b68f4015d6c033.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=3xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=y.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341276 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341277 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341278 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341279 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341280 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341280 13194139535279 true] [17592186341275 1110 #uuid "ef2a47c5-765b-5060-969a-a3cd21ed2b5b" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341276 1034 "y-x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341276 1111 #uuid "3bd2d98e-22f1-5e9f-8afa-ac68a4546b04" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341277 1034 "3x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341277 1111 #uuid "ca6aab5c-9410-5286-9b89-0c2cf34300f9" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341278 1034 "-y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341278 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341278 1111 #uuid "3c393ebe-ffae-5357-b5c2-2303d5da7402" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341279 1034 "x-y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341279 1111 #uuid "dd3794f3-c6de-538b-89c7-61ce2df8986a" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341280 1034 "y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341280 1111 #uuid "df266f08-0254-5796-a91e-32108a90b5ca" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341282 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1089 "q-109112" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1100 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1113 #uuid "c4b2f939-bcbe-57d3-adca-dd40d50dfdce" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341281 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341283 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341284 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341285 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341286 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341287 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341286 13194139535279 true] [17592186341282 1110 #uuid "67d0db4d-04ec-55b8-8db6-941083cf71e1" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341283 1034 "{x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341283 1111 #uuid "15419b8a-1912-5436-a3ab-3230480a2116" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341284 1034 "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-2x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341284 1111 #uuid "79570551-0734-5c0b-8c2d-44ca8d81412c" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341285 1034 "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341285 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341285 1111 #uuid "270a3a57-e22d-55c3-9334-4e421e0c0f16" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341286 1034 "{x}^{2}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341286 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341286 1111 #uuid "4509c12b-f309-54bd-91a6-b746f47caa94" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341287 1034 "{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341287 1111 #uuid "7ebd8e4f-93fd-596a-9767-e04e33363c61" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341289 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1089 "q-109114" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1100 "Consider the quadrilateral $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1113 #uuid "373bf35a-f882-57a8-91c1-93858fadb2fc" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341288 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/b7fad52bdf54c0870ad4c4939a314f5657e390b589faf76e60a8d8ef5d926039.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy{e}^{xy}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,dx+2xy{e}^{xy}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[(2y+2x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}-(2y+x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2x{y}^{2}-2y-x{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341290 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341291 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341292 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341293 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341294 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341292 13194139535279 true] [17592186341289 1110 #uuid "6de1b93d-bbc6-54ab-af34-9e4b5c72294a" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341290 1034 "(2x-y){e}^{xy}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341290 1111 #uuid "fd4a919d-2d44-59f8-be2c-6165664e9787" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341291 1034 "(xy-{y}^{2}){e}^{xy}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341291 1111 #uuid "33f77250-8ade-50a1-9bce-84e0919b6223" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341292 1034 "x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341292 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341292 1111 #uuid "2dfe4906-c610-55c5-a8a4-83f3f7ee80af" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341293 1034 "-x{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341293 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341293 1111 #uuid "cb091942-7d84-5db1-a4b5-2095dbda37b8" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341294 1034 "(y-2x){e}^{xy}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341294 1111 #uuid "4d073f60-8d10-5832-b93b-ff0d4fa83872" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341296 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1089 "q-109115" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1113 #uuid "f3566a7d-b3b7-5dcf-bd29-b00dd8bc7628" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341295 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341297 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341298 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341299 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341300 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341301 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341301 13194139535279 true] [17592186341296 1110 #uuid "4de0cfeb-c8e2-5ab1-99a2-9d0e011273b5" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341297 1034 "-7" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341297 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341297 1111 #uuid "b5b9b594-f3fb-5769-83c2-0dc1ada2556b" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341298 1034 "8" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341298 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341298 1111 #uuid "79155285-d146-53af-8186-5ff16e207459" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341299 1034 "-\\frac{15}{4}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341299 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341299 1111 #uuid "e700e60c-e611-565f-b9c2-7b192979a68f" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341300 1034 "\\frac{11}{2}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341300 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341300 1111 #uuid "df0b064a-66d1-5598-b021-2c4ea2befcdd" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341301 1034 "4" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341301 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341301 1111 #uuid "b7f89eba-5dc7-5dcb-ad23-c65b74b7bf03" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341303 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1089 "q-109160" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1113 #uuid "fb32a8d9-aa89-5e27-976f-eafd97e6f9c3" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341302 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341304 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341305 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341306 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341307 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341308 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341306 13194139535279 true] [17592186341303 1110 #uuid "e0ad572f-e943-5498-a564-8675655daeba" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341304 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341304 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341304 1111 #uuid "04b7e7e3-ca78-50e0-809f-8b3d3d03a910" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341305 1034 "-\\frac{7}{3}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341305 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341305 1111 #uuid "d7d6a01e-c34f-5983-adf9-290b8f1ac849" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341306 1034 "4" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341306 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341306 1111 #uuid "67e72b99-1de1-51ec-9cb8-fc62b3796b3f" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341307 1034 "0" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341307 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341307 1111 #uuid "c4468424-e400-5904-a99b-b17758f9a7b6" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341308 1034 "-2" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341308 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341308 1111 #uuid "39bf819b-a038-5d9f-8d3b-86af1f7a241f" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341310 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1089 "q-109164" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1113 #uuid "5a6dc375-a6eb-5992-aad2-4ef3ba82fede" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341309 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341311 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341312 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341313 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341314 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341315 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341314 13194139535279 true] [17592186341310 1110 #uuid "5229ec56-3f0b-5fa8-86d4-129290e5188e" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341311 1034 "-9" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341311 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341311 1111 #uuid "ffa389a3-d361-5ae0-bea8-234757030082" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341312 1034 "\\frac{23}{5}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341312 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341312 1111 #uuid "b82b0bdc-b930-5743-8902-204960832819" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341313 1034 "6" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341313 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341313 1111 #uuid "80a6444e-fc55-5591-b399-ca0688eae1e3" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341314 1034 "-\\frac{81}{10}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341314 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341314 1111 #uuid "3666d6cc-e324-547c-a228-4aea9f7581f7" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341315 1034 "\\frac{41}{20}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341315 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341315 1111 #uuid "5f153383-2fe2-58b7-9b84-bab4e991f500" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341317 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1089 "q-109254" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1100 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the unit circle and the coordinate axes in the first quadrant. Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1113 #uuid "ec0b1748-e212-5d96-8358-0b705e724a9f" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341316 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e48034aed1f41cf1f9b30c057186879233fe05cd2ba720dcee436398a732b6ce.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(x-{e}^{-{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341318 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341319 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341320 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341321 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341322 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341322 13194139535279 true] [17592186341317 1110 #uuid "52c07f15-4e19-51a7-aa31-e2ea2ff17928" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341318 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341318 1111 #uuid "eec591a5-9725-59ba-9f3e-155f090f1f81" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341319 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341319 1111 #uuid "419f7d57-8076-5b50-a685-73ebcce09f5d" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341320 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341320 1111 #uuid "d92f9b00-e280-587b-8f88-a00f06b8448c" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341321 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341321 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341321 1111 #uuid "2c9c4389-a42f-5092-94ba-85897e5a9c73" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341322 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341322 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341322 1111 #uuid "09b8c366-f7ae-56ee-a696-f10a9efb7c28" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341324 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1089 "q-109263" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1100 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1113 #uuid "ee280347-8c9a-5f0b-8ae2-75a94f990bab" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341323 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341325 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341326 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341327 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341328 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341329 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341327 13194139535279 true] [17592186341324 1110 #uuid "991b5390-3ab8-5b4d-9c84-44b1646d58ec" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341325 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341325 1111 #uuid "ac6ceaee-4a2d-50e4-832a-caa63d321684" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341326 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}(2y-x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341326 1111 #uuid "71d6643f-530b-53ef-b345-a0a3bd6a9d1b" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341327 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341327 1111 #uuid "f0e36259-d784-5470-a4ef-51a121d23e58" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341328 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341328 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341328 1111 #uuid "8a233259-195c-5ede-aba6-03b683d14b49" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341329 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+2}^{{x}^{2}+x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341329 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341329 1111 #uuid "875b9ea1-38d2-5e39-88c4-1e7ceec402ed" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341331 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1089 "q-109291" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1113 #uuid "d02b88c9-898d-50c4-a59e-b0f309ffc0a2" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341330 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341332 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341333 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341334 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341335 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341336 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341334 13194139535279 true] [17592186341331 1110 #uuid "f53d5d9f-7c77-5108-b98b-9fc856f7cb89" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341332 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341332 1111 #uuid "65855edd-ff16-5542-885c-f4ba18b44d2e" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341333 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(y+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341333 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341333 1111 #uuid "46feb382-e198-53ec-846e-167020db95a5" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341334 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341334 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341334 1111 #uuid "54566b29-f04f-55e0-b35a-9fdd7cb331aa" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341335 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x+1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341335 1111 #uuid "1a738a4d-8566-5bdb-a6e3-86db9d0c235e" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341336 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}(x-(2+{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }dy\\,dx" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341336 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341336 1111 #uuid "d7ca2fc9-7023-5282-aab8-0ff034b70973" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341338 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1089 "q-41811" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1113 #uuid "02c2afcf-c82c-531e-a25b-5a0c4c7e8242" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341337 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341339 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341340 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341341 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341342 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341343 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341341 13194139535279 true] [17592186341338 1110 #uuid "f283d133-f208-5ecf-94cb-b68e3328340c" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341339 1034 "-\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341339 1111 #uuid "e2a82101-c28b-5830-81af-b93c42572664" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341340 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341340 1111 #uuid "023a78d9-3652-5259-a852-292d93e71fb1" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341341 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341341 1111 #uuid "b70c91ed-7e50-5684-aa1a-ce4f0f37fc33" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341342 1034 "\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341342 1111 #uuid "57efe768-472c-5f2c-8dba-cab3a5ca79b1" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341343 1034 "-\\frac{8}{3}" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341343 1111 #uuid "dcc584ed-5d2b-5c0f-a536-3a70bc8a2bfd" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341345 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1089 "q-42107" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1113 #uuid "95adc28f-4c53-5d2d-9483-596d09977402" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341344 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061381/assets/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341346 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341347 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341348 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341349 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341350 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1041 "selection" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341346 13194139535279 true] [17592186341345 1110 #uuid "7860034a-ceac-5639-8545-deba4c73609b" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341346 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341346 1111 #uuid "0f36e145-58db-55be-ad3e-95fd323b3ffe" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341347 1034 "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535279 true] [17592186341347 1111 #uuid "e322714e-cf89-5b37-9882-8400000ffb80" 13194139535279 true] [17592186341348 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorn